<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Zhizhong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Zhizhong was a Chinese Nationalist general who commanded in the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1937 and later became a leading negotiator with the Communists. As head of the Nationalist administration in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1946, he negotiated a coalition government for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> with the leaders of the Second East Turkestan Republic. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> for peace talks in 1949, he remained there and served the People's Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zhizhong.jpg" alt="Zhang Zhizhong" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; scanned by Internet Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Zhizhong2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhang Zhizhong, also written Chang Chih-chung, was born on 27 October 1890 in Chaohu, Anhui. He graduated from the Baoding Military Academy as an infantry officer in 1916 and briefly studied social sciences at Shanghai University without taking a degree. After serving in the warlord armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> he answered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s call and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> as an instructor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a>, and he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>. He belonged to the left wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, favouring cooperation with the Communists against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the nationalisation of foreign-owned businesses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1929 to 1937 he was Commandant of the Central Military Academy, charged with shaping the beliefs of young officer candidates, and he was counted among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s trusted confidants. He fought in Chiang's campaign against Yan Xishan and Feng Yuxiang, led the 5th Army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1932 and helped set up the air defences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. In 1937, at the head of the 9th Army Group, he supervised the defence of Shanghai.</p><p>As Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> from 1937 to 1940 he was responsible for the Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, which ran out of control after he ordered key buildings razed before an expected Japanese attack; he was relieved, and several men blamed for the faulty intelligence were executed. Chiang nonetheless made him Director of the Political Department of the Military Affairs Commission in 1940. He was Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1946 and 1947, dealing throughout with the Ili Rebellion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the failed peace talks of 1949 he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and persuaded his friend General Tao Zhiyue, the Nationalist governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, to defect, which greatly helped the mostly peaceful incorporation of Xinjiang into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Mao Zedong made him Vice Chairman of the Northwest China Military and Political Committee, of the National Defense Council from 1954 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> from 1965. Protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>, he escaped persecution in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, and he died in April 1969, aged 78.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Zhizhong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1946 it joined a coalition provincial government with the Nationalist government, negotiated by <b>Zhang Zhizhong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-east-turkestan-republic">Second East Turkestan Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chaohu, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.6,117.87&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Chaohu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zhang Zhizhong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4845 4862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4845 4862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.6,117.87&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
